        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Revolutions of 1848</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Where</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the Italian states, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire</a> (including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and the Czech lands) and the states of the German Confederation; smaller risings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, the Romanian Principalities and Ireland; echoes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">revolution in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> January 1848; February Revolution in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the March risings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>; counter-revolution from May to December 1848; a second wave in 1849 ending with Hungary's surrender in August and the fall of Komárom in October 1849</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Leading figures</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-philippe-i">Louis Philippe I</a>, the French king who abdicated; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klemens-von-metternich">Klemens von Metternich</a>, Austrian chancellor, who resigned; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lajos-kossuth">Lajos Kossuth</a>, leader of the Hungarian government; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-mazzini">Giuseppe Mazzini</a>, organiser of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/young-italy">Young Italy</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-william-iv-of-prussia">Frederick William IV of Prussia</a>, who refused the imperial crown; Louis Napoléon Bonaparte, elected French president in December 1848</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">most revolutions were suppressed, tens of thousands were killed and more went into exile; lasting reforms included the end of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, the end of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/absolute-monarchy">absolute monarchy</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> and representative democracy in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a></span></div>
