    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>More than fifty countries were affected by the wave of revolutions between January 1848 and October 1849, but without significant coordination or cooperation among their revolutionaries.</li><li>Coalitions of workers and reformers, including members of the middle and upper classes, won early concessions in the spring of 1848, then divided; conservative forces and armies suppressed most of the revolutions, killing tens of thousands of people.</li><li>Many of the revolutionaries' goals were achieved within about two decades, largely by their opponents: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a> ended in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, and Hungary gained more self-determination in 1867.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>When news of the revolution in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, the Austrian chancellor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klemens-von-metternich">Klemens von Metternich</a> is said to have exclaimed, in French, &quot;well, my dear, it's all over!&quot;; a few weeks later he resigned and went into exile.</p></aside>
    </section>

