    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1848</span><span class="rf-text">A revolution in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, begins the wave of revolutions in January</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1848</span><span class="rf-text">King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-philippe-i">Louis Philippe I</a> abdicates on 24 February and the French Second Republic is proclaimed</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1848</span><span class="rf-text">Fighting in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> in March forces Metternich to resign, and barricade fighting in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> leads Frederick William IV to withdraw the army</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1848</span><span class="rf-text">The June Days uprising of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> workers is suppressed by General Cavaignac, and Louis Napoléon Bonaparte is elected French president in December</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1849</span><span class="rf-text">King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-william-iv-of-prussia">Frederick William IV of Prussia</a> rejects the imperial crown offered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt-parliament">Frankfurt National Assembly</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1849</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> falls to French intervention in July and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> surrenders in August</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1849</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> surrenders to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> on 13 August, and the fortress of Komárom falls in October</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/french-revolution.jpg" alt="French Revolution" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anonymous_-_Prise_de_la_Bastille.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unidentified painter, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The revolutions grew out of the short- and long-term changes brought by industrialisation and out of the political legacy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>: rapid population growth, urbanisation, a repressive political order set up in reaction to the French Revolution, and the spread of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberalism">liberalism</a>, radicalism and nationalism. In the countryside peasants had lost customary rights to common land and forests through enclosure and privatisation; in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavarian-palatinate">Bavarian Palatinate</a> those convicted of wood theft rose from 37,500 in 1821–22 to 185,000 in 1846–47. The largest pre-revolutionary peasant uprising against feudal lords took place in Austrian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> in 1846.</p><p>Urban workers met oversupplied labour markets and falling real wages. Most workers in 1848 were skilled artisans, whom mechanisation threatened. Educated men found too few careers: Wilhelm Heinrich Riehl wrote of an "intellectual <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/proletariat">proletariat</a>" in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and many who failed in their professions turned to journalism and later led the revolutions.</p><p>According to the economic historians Helge Berger and Mark Spoerer, the most immediate cause was the economic crisis of 1845 to 1847. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potato">Potato</a> blight devastated harvests from 1845, and the Great Famine directly killed over an eighth of Ireland's population, over 1 million of 8 million. Food riots followed, over 400 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1846–47 and 164 in the German states in 1847, and an industrial recession came in 1847. Berger and Spoerer found a strong correlation between the countries most deeply hit by it and those that had a revolution in 1848.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aims">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aims</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The revolutions varied widely in their aims but generally opposed conservative systems such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/absolute-monarchy">absolute monarchy</a> and feudalism and sought nation states founded on constitutionalism and popular <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>. Three currents opposed the governments. Liberals supported equality before the law, civil liberties including the right to own property, and constitutions; they preferred <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a> to absolute monarchy or radical republic, and generally sought a franchise restricted to male property-owners. Radicals, a loose coalition of democrats and socialists, wanted universal manhood suffrage and gave greater weight to the "social question" of the working poor. Radical democrats generally favoured regulation and state intervention, while socialists sought the abolition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">capitalism</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nationalism">Nationalism</a> promoted the unity and primacy of people bound by common language, culture, religion, history and geography. Workers demanded the right of association: in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> through cooperatives, mutual benefit societies and early trade unions, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> partly through a return to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guild</a> system.</p><p>Reformers across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> demanded a representative parliament, freedom of the press, freedom of association, universal manhood suffrage and a national guard. The Hungarian 12 points of 15 March 1848 added an independent Hungarian ministry, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jury-trial">trial by jury</a>, a Hungarian army, civil and religious equality and union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a>. In Wallachia the Proclamation of Islaz announced reforms including autonomy and the abolition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="events">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Course of Events</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-philippe-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-philippe-i.jpg" alt="Louis Philippe I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-philippe-i">Louis Philippe I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1841_portrait_painting_of_Louis_Philippe_I_(King_of_the_French)_by_Winterhalter.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Franz Xaver Winterhalter, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>A revolution in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a> in January 1848 began the wave, but the revolution in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in February held far greater import among contemporaries. After protests in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 22 February and the army's massacre of 65 demonstrators on 23 February, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-philippe-i">Louis Philippe I</a> abdicated on 24 February. Within weeks there were uprisings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buda">Buda</a> and Pest, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>. Governments mostly capitulated without significant resistance: Metternich resigned after violent clashes in Vienna from 13 to 15 March, and in Berlin, after barricade fighting on 18 March, Frederick William IV ordered the army to withdraw. Elections under broad franchises followed, including to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt-parliament">Frankfurt National Assembly</a>.</p><p>Divisions soon appeared. In France the Constituent Assembly elected on 23 April had a conservative majority, and the closing of the national workshops on 21 June led to the June Days uprising, in which 40,000 to 50,000 insurgents fought on the barricades of Paris until General Cavaignac suppressed it with significant bloodshed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> forces bombarded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> in June, Radetzky defeated the Piedmontese at Custoza in July, and Jelačić and Windisch-Grätz crushed the Vienna Uprising on 31 October.</p><p>In 1849 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> was beaten again at Novara in March. Frederick William IV rejected the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> crown, and the Imperial Constitution campaign in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> ended in defeat in July. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> fell in July and Venice surrendered in August. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, invaded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, surrendered on 13 August, and the last holdouts at Komárom gave in between 2 and 5 October.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="people">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">People</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-eugene-cavaignac"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-eugene-cavaignac.jpg" alt="Louis-Eugène Cavaignac" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-eugene-cavaignac">Louis-Eugène Cavaignac</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:G%C3%A9n%C3%A9ral_Cavaignac_-_photo_Pierre_Petit.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Pierre Petit, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-philippe-i">Louis Philippe I</a> was the king who abdicated, Louis Blanc, a socialist in the provisional government, led the Luxembourg Commission, and General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-eugene-cavaignac">Louis-Eugène Cavaignac</a> suppressed the June Days. Louis Napoléon Bonaparte, nephew of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>, won the presidential election of 10 December 1848 with 75 percent of the vote, with conservatives, peasants and urban workers backing him for different reasons; in December 1851 he launched a coup d'état, and the following year he took the name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-mazzini">Giuseppe Mazzini</a> led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/young-italy">Young Italy</a> movement within the larger Young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> network, whose methods contemporaries widely admired and imitated. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> lands Metternich resigned and went into exile, Ferdinand I abdicated in December 1848 in favour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-joseph-i">Franz Joseph I</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lajos-kossuth">Lajos Kossuth</a> led the Hungarian government. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sandor-petofi">Sándor Petőfi</a>'s poetry was read aloud with the 12 points in Pest. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frantisek-palacky">František Palacký</a> propagated Austroslavism, which sought greater autonomy for the Czech lands within the monarchy.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-hecker">Friedrich Hecker</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-struve">Gustav Struve</a> led a radical republican uprising in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> in April 1848, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archduke-john-of-austria">Archduke John of Austria</a> served as Imperial Regent, and Robert Blum was executed by Austrian troops. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-william-iv-of-prussia">Frederick William IV of Prussia</a> refused the crown. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Karl Marx</a> was expelled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> in early March 1848 on accusations of having helped arm Belgian revolutionaries.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="outcomes">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Outcomes</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/franz-joseph-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/franz-joseph-i.jpg" alt="Franz Joseph I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/franz-joseph-i">Franz Joseph I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Franz_Joseph_I_of_Austria.1910_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Carl Pietzner, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Many of the revolutions were quickly suppressed, tens of thousands of people were killed and even more were forced into exile. By the end of 1848 decidedly conservative governments had come to power across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>: Frederick William IV dismissed his liberal ministers and imposed a conservative constitution, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-joseph-i">Franz Joseph I</a>'s government dissolved the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a> in March 1849 and issued the March Constitution. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, the leading rebels such as Kossuth went into exile or were executed, among them former prime minister Batthyány and the Thirteen Martyrs of Arad. Governments partly reversed the reforms; arrests in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> territories rose from 70,000 in 1850 to one million by 1854, and censorship tightened in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>.</p><p>Some reforms lasted. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">Serfdom</a> was abolished in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire</a> by a patent of 7 September 1848, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/absolute-monarchy">absolute monarchy</a> ended in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> altered their constitutions. Tensions between bourgeois revolutionaries and radicals played a major role in the failure of the revolutions, and national conflicts, among Germans, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechs">Czechs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyars</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanians">Romanians</a>, divided the revolutionaries.</p><p>Many historians long considered the revolutions a failure, given the lack of visible structural change after 1848. Christopher Clark characterises the following period as one dominated by a revolution in government, as states managed the public sphere more effectively. Priscilla Robertson considered many of the revolutionaries' goals achieved by the 1870s, though largely by the enemies of the revolutions.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/karl-marx.jpg" alt="Karl Marx" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Karl Marx</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karl_Marx_001_restored.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">John Jabez Edwin Mayall, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Interpretations differ. Liberal democrats looked to 1848 as a democratic revolution that in the long run ensured liberty, equality and fraternity. For nationalists it was the springtime of hope, though the results fell short of what many had hoped. Communists denounced it as a betrayal of working-class ideals by an indifferent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bourgeoisie">bourgeoisie</a>, and the view of 1848 as a bourgeois revolution is also common in non-Marxist scholarship. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Karl Marx</a> expressed disappointment at its bourgeois character and in 1850 set out a theory of permanent revolution.</p><p>After the revolutions <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> abolished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a> in 1861, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> kept the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/universal-suffrage">universal male suffrage</a> the Second Republic had set up, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire</a> was reorganised into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Dual Monarchy</a> through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-compromise-of-1867">Ausgleich</a> of 1867, spearheaded by the former revolutionaries <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyula-andrassy">Gyula Andrássy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferenc-deak">Ferenc Deák</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> centrist coalitions such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camillo-benso-count-of-cavour">Cavour</a>'s Connubio took power.</p><p>Disillusioned and persecuted revolutionaries, above all from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and the Austrian Empire, went into exile and became known as the Forty-Eighters. About 4,000 went to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carl-schurz">Carl Schurz</a>; German American sentiment was largely anti-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, especially among the Forty-Eighters. The German historian Reinhard Rürup described the revolutions as a turning point in the development of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antisemitism">antisemitism</a>, as conspiracy theories presented <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> as representing both social revolution and international capital.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Songs in history</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/songs/poland-is-not-yet-lost">Poland Is Not Yet Lost<span>1797</span></a><a href="/songs/canto-degli-italiani">Il Canto degli italiani<span>1847</span></a></p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The revolutions of 1848 were a wave of uprisings across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> between January 1848 and October 1849 against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/absolute-monarchy">absolute monarchy</a> and feudalism. Coalitions of workers and reformers won early concessions but soon divided, and most revolutions were suppressed, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a> ended in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>.</em></p>

